{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And parched grain.</b> Flour made from tender grain (כַּרְמֶל) that they dry in the oven." "<b>And tender grain.</b> These are parched grains — שֶׁקּוֹרִין גרניי\"ליש." "<b>In all your dwellings.</b> The Sages of Israel disagreed about this. Some learn from here that the prohibition of new grain applies outside the Land, and others say it comes only to teach that they were not commanded about new grain until after inheritance and settlement — once they conquered and divided (Ketubot 37)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {1 "the la'az גרניי\"ליש is Old French grain(s) tendre(s) or similar, 'tender grains'", 2 "the Talmudic source is Ketubot 37b, not Kiddushin 37; the Hebrew citation abbreviation may read either קידושין or קדושין, but the actual passage is in Ketubot"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:57.349712818Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}